# Tags that must publish nothing.
#
# A tombstone tag exists only to carry a `retract` directive in its go.mod. Go
# reads retractions from the highest version, so retracting a withdrawn release
# means publishing a tag above it -- and that tag must not become a release,
# must not take `latest`, and must not push an image, or it undoes the very
# withdrawal it was cut to protect.
#
# One tag per line, exact match, `#` comments ignored. The release workflow
# reads this file at the tag being released. An empty list is valid and means
# every tag publishes normally.
#
# This used to be inferred: the workflow asked whether a tag retracted its own
# version, on the reasoning that nothing else would. That stopped being true the
# moment a *real* release had to be retracted for Go consumers while still
# shipping an image, a chart and binaries -- v1.0.0-rc.1 is exactly that, and
# under the old rule it would have published nothing at all. Being listed is a
# decision; retracting yourself is now just a fact about go.mod.
#
# The list is empty as of 2026-09-07. It held v1.7.4 and v1.8.0; both tags were
# deleted once the retract block moved onto the release tags themselves, so
# neither named anything that existed. See the comment above `retract` in
# go.mod. Re-list a tag here before pushing one that must publish nothing.
